  <title>6.1.2: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01570</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.1.2: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7a38b2ed-90c4-4b17-9644-6c53fd8ea531</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>We will solve these two problems as a class.</para>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Do these terms have the same variables with the same exponents? Explain your answer. 
        
        \(6x^4y^2\)         \(8x^4y^2\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>Yes. Both terms have the same variables raised to the same degrees: \(x\) is raised to the fourth power, and \(y\) is squared.</para>

</solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Add the terms together: \(6x^4y^2+8x^4y^2\). What is the sum? 
    </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(14x^4y^2\)</para>

</solution></exercise>

<para>In problems 3 - 7, simplify the monomial addition and subtraction problems on your own. Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work.</para>

<!--Q#3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(12q+9q\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(21q\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Q#4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(-14c^3+5c^3\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(-9c^3\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Q#5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(8g^2h^2+(-6g^2h^2)\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(2g^2h^2\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(8mn^3p-(-5mn^3p)\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(13mn^3p\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Q#7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\(-16a^2+3b^2+8a^2 \)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(-8a^2+3b^2\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Can the following monomials be simplified using like terms? Explain. 
        
        \(17a^2bc^3+6a^2b^2c^3\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>No, the monomial terms do not contain the same variables with the same exponents. The first term has the variable \(b\) with an exponent of 1. The second term has the variable \(b\) with an exponent of 2. The monomials are not like terms.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<para>In problems 9 - 14, simplify the polynomial addition and subtraction problems on your own.  Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work.</para>

<!--Q#9-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\( (5k+7m-2)+(7k+9m-4)\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(12k+16m-6\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 


<!--Q#10-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((12w-11)-(5w+6) \)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(7w-5\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 


<!--Q#11-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((5k^2+7k-2)+(7k^2+9k-4)\)</para>
    <para>You can use the following video if you need some help.</para>
    
    <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ba5bc330fb9f7f706e35cf5a38cdfedb85f34aec"/></media><para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(12k^2+16k-6\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 


<!--Q#12-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((9h^2-4h+17)+(2h^2-9)\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(11h^2-4h+8\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 




<!--Q#13-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((8f^2+6fv-3v^2)-(6f^2-3v^2)\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(2f^2+6fv\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --> 


<!--Q#14-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>\((8z^3-6zx+3x^2)+(12z^3-4zx)\)</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
