</section>

<!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
<section>
  <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
    <section>    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>

<!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    
    <para>Simplify. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
    
    <para>\((6a^3-2a^2b)-2(ab^2+3b^3)+(2a^2b+2ab^2)\)</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(6a^3-6b^3\)</para>
    
    <para>Here is how to solve this problem:
      \((6a^3-2a^2b)-2(ab^2+3b^3)+(2a^2b+2ab^2)\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 - 
     </emphasis> Distribute the multiplication over addition.</para>
    
    <para>     \(6a^3-2a^2b-2ab^2-6b^3+2a^2b+2ab^2\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 - 
     </emphasis> Use the Commutative Property to rearrange like terms together.</para>
    
    <para>     \(6a^3-2a^2b+2a^2b-2ab^2+2ab^2-6b^3\)</para>
    
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 - 
     </emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
    
    <para>     \(6a^3-6b^3\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction--></section>
</section>


<note class="self-check"><para>Find the sum.</para>
  <para>\((14y^2+6y-4)+(3y^2+8y+5)\)</para></note>

  
<section><title>Additional Resources</title>
  <section><title>Adding and Subtracting Monomials</title>
    <para>We have learned how to simplify expressions by combining like terms. Remember, like terms must have the same variables with the same exponent. Since monomials are terms, adding and subtracting monomials is the same as combining like terms. If the monomials are like terms, we just combine them by adding or subtracting the coefficients. Remember that like terms must have the same variables with the same exponents.</para>
    <para>These terms have the same variables and the same exponents:</para>
    <list>
      <item>\(5m^3n^2\)          \(2m^3n^2\)</item>
    </list>
    <para>So we can add the monomials:</para>
    <list>
      <item>\(5m^3n^2 + 2m^3n^2 = 7m^3n^2\)</item>
    </list>
    <para>The variables and exponents of the monomials do not change when we add or subtract them.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
    <para>\(18a+2a\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
    <para>\(18a+2a = 20a\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
    <para>\(37t-15t\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
    <para>\(37t-15t =22t\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
    <para>\(25y^2+15y^2\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
    <para>\(25y^2+15y^2=40y^2\)</para>
    
    
    <note class="try">
    <title>Try It: Adding and Subtracting Monomials</title>
    
    <exercise>
      <problem>
          
        <para>\(16pq^3-(-7pq^3)\)</para>
      
      <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>Compare your answer:</para>
      
      <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}16pq^3-(-7pq^3)&amp;=&amp;16pq^3+7pq^3\\&amp;=&amp;23pq^3\end{array}\)</para>
    
    </solution></exercise></note>
    <!--Interaction End --> 
    
    
    <para>We can think of adding and subtracting polynomials as just adding and subtracting a series of monomials. Look for the like terms—those with the same variables and the same exponent. The Commutative Property allows us to rearrange the terms to put like terms together.</para>
    <para>Let’s look at some examples.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
    <para>Find the sum: \((7y^2-2y+9)+(4y^2-8y-7)\).</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify like terms by finding those with the same variable and same exponent.</para>
    <para>\(({\style{color:red}7}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} - {\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y} + 9) + ({\style{color:red}4}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} - {\style{color:blue}8}{\style{color:blue}y} - 7)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Use the Commutative Property to rearrange like terms together. Be sure to take the appropriate sign with each term \((+/-)\).</para>
    <para>\({\style{color:red}7}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} + {\style{color:red}4}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} - {\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y} - {\style{color:blue}8}{\style{color:blue}y} + 9 - 7\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
    <para>\({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2} - {\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}0}{\style{color:blue}y} + 2\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
    <para>Find the sum: \((22g-14h+3)+(10g+8h-9)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify like terms.</para>
    <para>\(({\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}h}+3)+({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}0}{\style{color:red}g}+{\style{color:blue}8}{\style{color:blue}h}-9)\)</para>
    <para>Color 22g, 10g in red.  14h, 8h in blue.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Use the Commutative Property to rearrange like terms together. Be sure to take the appropriate sign with each term (+/−).</para>
    <para>\(({\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}g}+{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}0}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}h}+{\style{color:blue}8}{\style{color:blue}h} +3+9)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
    <para>\(32g-6h +12\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
    <para>Find the sum: \((8j+7k)+(2s^2-3k+26)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify like terms.</para>
    <para>\((8j+{\style{color:blue}7}{\style{color:blue}k}-3)+({\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}s}^{\style{color:red}2}-{\style{color:blue}3}{\style{color:blue}k}+26)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Rearrange like terms together.</para>
    <para>\({\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}s}^{\style{color:red}2}+{\style{color:blue}7}{\style{color:blue}k}-{\style{color:blue}3}{\style{color:blue}k}+8j-3+26\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Example 4</emphasis></para>
    <para>Find the difference: \((15g-4h+8)-(10g+13h-1)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify like terms.</para>
    <para>\(({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}h}+8)-({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}0}{\style{color:red}g}+{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}3}{\style{color:blue}h}-1)\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Rearrange like terms together.</para>
    <para>Remember to distribute the subtraction to all terms in the second polymnomial.</para>
    <para>\({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}0}{\style{color:red}g}-{\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}h}-{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}3}{\style{color:blue}h}+8+1\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
    <para>\(5g-17h+9\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Example 5</emphasis></para>
    <para>Find the difference: \((16y^2+12y+9)-(3y^2+2y-12) \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Identify like terms.</para>
    <para>\(({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}6}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}+{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}+9)-({\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}+{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}-12) \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Rearrange like terms together.</para>
    <para>\(({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}6}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}-{\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}+{\style{color:blue}1}{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}-{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}+9+12) \)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Combine like terms.</para>
    <para>\((13y^2+10y+21) \)</para>
    
    <note class="try">
    <title>Try It: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</title>
    
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <para>Find the sum and difference. If necessary, be careful with the signs as you distribute while subtracting the polynomials.</para>
        
        <para>\((7x^2-4x+5)+(x^2-7x+3)\)</para>
        <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>Compare your answer:</para>
      
      <para>\((7x^2-4x+5)+(x^2-7x+3)=8x^2-11x+8\)</para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
  </note>
    <!--Q#2b-->
    <!--Interaction End --> 
    
    <!--Q#3c-->
    <!--Interaction End --> 
    
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <para>\((8a+12b)-(10a+3b)\)</para>
        <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>Compare your answer:</para>
      
      <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}(8a+12b)-(10a+3b)&amp;=&amp;8a+12b-10a-3b\\&amp;=&amp;-2a+9b\\&amp;=&amp;2a-9b\end{array}\)</para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
    <exercise>
      <problem>
        
        <para>\((9w^2-7w+5)-(2w^2-4)\)</para>
        <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution>
      <para>Compare your answer:</para>
      
      <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}(9w^2-7w+5)-(2w^2-4)&amp;=&amp;(9w^2-7w+5)-2w^2+4\\&amp;=&amp;7w^2-7w+9\end{array}\)</para>
    
    </solution></exercise>
    </section></section>
